  "text": "The spinal withdrawal reflex illustrates a circuit organizing response. A painful stimulus stimulates the firing of sensory neurons whose signals enter the spinal cord. These sensory neurons send signals to the spinal cord. Input can excite motor neurons supplying flexor muscles through spinal interneurons, resulting in a pattern that facilitates the withdrawal of the affected limb. Inhibition of opposing muscle motor neurons contributes to movement. Inhibition does not always mean the whole behavior stops. A spinal response can begin before conscious awareness of the stimulus. Ascending pathways carry information toward the brain, while descending signals from the brain can modify spinal activity. The reflex demonstrates local circuit processing within a nervous system. The nervous system has regions whose regions remain connected and influence one another.",
  "reason": "The text was constructed by sequentially linking concepts via their defined relations in source order: starting with the reflex illustrating the response, moving through stimulus-sensory-spinal pathways, then interneuron-mediated excitation and withdrawal, followed by inhibition effects, timing relative to consciousness, brain communication (ascending/descending), and finally structural nervous system properties. Modality qualifiers ('can', 'does not always') and argument qualifiers were preserved as specified in the relation definitions."
